# Environment variables THIS package reads. None of them ever reaches the
# browser — nothing in src/ reads import.meta.env.
#
# ── TWO KINDS, and the difference decides where a value has to be set ──
# BUILD-TIME: read once while the site is built, by scripts/build-content.ts and
# vite.config.ts. RUNTIME: read on every request by the form endpoints
# (api/audit.ts, api/contact.ts, via server/config.ts) out of process.env.
# On Vercel both live on the same Settings screen. Locally they do NOT — see the
# mail block, which `.env` does not reach.
#
# ── This file is a TEMPLATE. Nothing loads it. ──
# Copy it to `.env` and put real values there; `.env` is gitignored and this
# one is the documented exception. Editing `.env.example` itself has no effect
# on any build — it is documentation that happens to be valid shell syntax.
# `npm run content:build` loads `.env` via `node --env-file-if-exists`.
#
# None of these is required locally: with nothing set, `npm run content:build`
# reads content/fixtures/ offline.
#
# ── The Studio has its OWN .env, and its own variable names ──
# `sanity-studio/.env`, holding SANITY_STUDIO_PROJECT_ID and
# SANITY_STUDIO_DATASET. The Sanity CLI loads .env from the Studio directory
# and exposes ONLY the SANITY_STUDIO_ prefix, so the names below are invisible
# to it. Setting them here will not start the Studio; see sanity-studio/README.md.
#
# The split is deliberate rather than accidental: SANITY_STUDIO_ marks a value
# as safe to compile into the Studio's browser bundle, and the variables below —
# SANITY_TOKEN in particular — must never carry a prefix that would put them there.
#
# Vercel: set these under Settings → Environment Variables,
# scoped per environment as noted. Never use a VITE_ prefix for a credential —
# Vite compiles VITE_* into the public bundle. That rule is now CHECKED rather
# than remembered: the build aborts on a VITE_-prefixed name that reads as a
# credential, and on any secret's value appearing in an emitted asset
# (scripts/secretScan.ts, wired up as assertNoSecrets in vite.config.ts).
#
# ── Variables set by Vercel, which you never create by hand ──
# VERCEL, VERCEL_ENV and VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL arrive from "Automatically
# expose System Environment Variables" and are absent from the list below on
# purpose. VERCEL_ENV must read exactly production, preview or development;
# anything else — a hand-typed override, a capitalised paste — fails the build
# and the form endpoint rather than being read as "not production".

# ── Content source (scripts/build-content.ts, ADR 010, ADR 011) ─────────────
# Sanity project id. Its presence selects the Sanity source.
#   Production: REQUIRED unless CONTENT_SOURCE=seed is set — a production build
#   with neither fails on purpose rather than shipping fixtures by omission.
#   Preview: optional (unset → fixtures, the client-demo content).
SANITY_PROJECT_ID=
# Sanity dataset, e.g. `production`. REQUIRED whenever the Sanity source is
# selected, in EVERY environment. Never defaulted: a guessed dataset is how a
# local build ends up reading the client's live content while looking offline.
SANITY_DATASET=
# Optional read-only token. Needed only if the dataset is private — published
# website content is already public, so a public dataset with no build token is
# the preferred arrangement (ADR 011). Drafts are excluded by the query, not by
# permissions. NEVER VITE_-prefixed.
SANITY_TOKEN=
# Per-request deadline in milliseconds (default 15000). Must be a positive
# number — an invalid value fails the build rather than falling back.
SANITY_TIMEOUT_MS=
# Force a source: sanity | fixture | seed. Overrides the default choice.
# `seed` builds from the committed content/seed/ snapshot, prints a banner, and
# exists only to ship a code-only change while the CMS is unavailable.
# The Sanity API version is NOT here: it decides response shape, so it is pinned
# in content/lib/sanity.ts and changes through a reviewed code change.
CONTENT_SOURCE=

# ── Build environment (vite.config.ts) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Local override to exercise the PRODUCTION path without Vercel: drops the
# debug console, emits robots.txt Allow + sitemap.xml, no noindex. On Vercel
# this is supplied as the system variable VERCEL_ENV — "Automatically expose
# System Environment Variables" MUST be enabled in the project; without it
# every deployment builds as development (debug console ships, robots says
# Disallow: /). VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL (canonical / og:url / sitemap
# origin) comes from the same toggle.
#
# ITS SCOPE IS THE CLIENT BUILD AND NOTHING ELSE. The content build reads
# VERCEL_ENV only, so a local VITE_VERCEL_ENV=production still builds fixtures —
# which is what makes it usable without Sanity credentials, and what stops it
# being a rehearsal of the production content rules. Same three values as
# VERCEL_ENV; anything else fails the build.
VITE_VERCEL_ENV=

# ── Mail (server/config.ts, api/audit.ts, api/contact.ts) ───────────────────
# RUNTIME, not build-time: read per request, so changing one on Vercel takes
# effect without a redeploy — and dropping one breaks a deployed site that
# builds fine.
#
# LOCALLY, `.env` DOES NOT REACH THESE. Vite does not load .env files into
# process.env, and `npm run dev` / `npm run preview` serve /api through the
# apiRouting plugin in vite.config.ts, which hands the handler process.env as it
# stands. Export them in the shell for the session if you need to test a real
# send; with nothing set the endpoints run in dry-run and the forms work end to
# end offline, which is the intended default on a fresh clone.
#
# The recipient is NOT here. It comes from the CMS (server/recipient.ts), so
# changing who gets an enquiry is a content edit, not a deployment variable.

# Resend API key. Its presence selects real sending; absent means dry-run —
# validated, rendered, logged, not sent.
#   Production: REQUIRED. Missing, the endpoint fails the request rather than
#   returning a receipt for mail nobody will get.
#   Preview / local: optional (unset → dry-run).
# NEVER VITE_-prefixed.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Sender, `Name <address@domain>` or a bare address, on a domain verified in
# Resend. REQUIRED whenever RESEND_API_KEY is set, and never guessed — a wrong
# sender is a Resend 403 that reads like an outage.
MAIL_FROM=
# Overrides the CMS recipient. REQUIRED while MAIL_FROM uses Resend's sandbox
# sender (@resend.dev), which delivers only to the Resend account owner — that
# pair without it is refused rather than 422ing on every submission. Otherwise a
# preview-only convenience.
MAIL_TO_OVERRIDE=
# Accept and answer without sending. Any value except `0` turns it on.
# REFUSED IN PRODUCTION: a dry run there is a form that reports success and
# sends nothing, the same lie CONTENT_SOURCE=fixture is refused for.
MAIL_DRY_RUN=
# Upstream deadline in ms (default 10000). Must be a positive number; an invalid
# value fails the request rather than falling back.
MAIL_TIMEOUT_MS=
# Per-hour submission caps (defaults 5 per IP, 3 per email address). Same rule:
# positive numbers only, invalid fails.
MAIL_RATE_PER_HOUR=
MAIL_RATE_EMAIL_PER_HOUR=

# ── Launch gate (middleware.ts, server/launchGate.ts) ────────────────────────
# RUNTIME. Until the premiere (2026-09-26 00:00 Madrid) every request gets a
# countdown. Visiting any URL with ?launch-key=<this value> sets a cookie that
# lets that browser see the real site early. At least 16 characters; shorter or
# unset, nobody gets through before the launch. NEVER VITE_-prefixed.
LAUNCH_PREVIEW_KEY=

# ── Never set in any deployment ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Escape hatch for inspecting a failing bundle locally. It disables BOTH chunk
# budgets AND the intro-standalone assertion in vite.config.ts.
# VERTIGO_SKIP_BUDGETS=1
